WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 204: The Babylonian Empire: Nebuchadnezzar's Dream, the Ishtar Gate, and the Hanging Gardens

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.600
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.800
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:11.040
[Rebecca] Today we enter the golden splendor of the Neo Babylonian Empire.

4
00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:18.720
[Rebecca] In the 6th century before Christ, Babylon was the intellectual, astronomical, and architectural capital of the ancient world.

5
00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.520
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story of Nebuchadnezzar I.

6
00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:41.360
[George] For centuries, the ancient city of Babylon, the historic home of Hammurabi's famous law code on the banks of the Euphrates River, had been sacked, burned, and subjugated by the brutal emperors of Assyria.

7
00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:54.160
[George] In 689 BC, Assyrian king Sennacherib had been so enraged by Babylonian rebellion that he diverted the Euphrates waters through the city streets, leveling its temples and cursing the ground.

8
00:00:54.160 --> 00:01:03.120
[George] But in 626 BC, an ambitious Chaldean military governor named Nabopallasar raised the banner of national rebellion.

9
00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:14.960
[George] Uniting with Syaxares, king of the Medes, Nabopolisar led a ferocious war of liberation, culminating in the historic capture and utter destruction of Nineveh in 612 BC.

10
00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:26.000
[George] From the ashes of Assyria, Nabopolisar founded the Neo-Babylonian Empire, declaring that Babylon would rise again to be the center of world power, culture, and science.

11
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:45.840
[George] He restored ancient irrigation canals, rebuilt protective walls, and placed his brilliant 25-year-old crown prince, Nebuchadnezzar, in command of the Imperial Armed Forces, tasked with driving the remnants of the Egyptian and Assyrian armies completely out of the Levant and securing Babylonian supremacy.

12
00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:57.600
[George] In the spring of 605 BC, the geopolitical destiny of the ancient Near East was decided on the banks of the upper Euphrates River at the historic Battle of Carchimish in modern day Turkey.

13
00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:07.960
[George] Pharaoh Necho Ii of Egypt had marched a massive Egyptian and Greek mercenary army north to support the remnants of the defeated Assyrians and block Babylonian expansion.

14
00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:22.440
[George] Crown Prince Nebuchadnezzar launched a lightning amphibious and land assault across the Euphrates, surrounding the Egyptian forces and shattering them in a ferocious battle celebrated in both the Babylonian Chronicles and the Biblical Book of Jeremiah.

15
00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:27.400
[George] Necho's army was rooted, fleeing south through Syria in total disarray.

16
00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:37.560
[George] While pursuing the remnants toward the Sinai border, an urgent royal courier arrived on a galloping horse with grave news King Nabapolisar had died in Babylon.

17
00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:50.040
[George] Recognizing that a moment's hesitation could invite rival court coups, Nebuchadnezzar made a daring strategic move, entrusting his heavy army and thousands of Syrian and Jewish captives to his generals.

18
00:02:50.040 --> 00:03:01.960
[George] He took a small retinue of horsemen and galloped across 500 miles of waterless desert in just fourteen days, arriving in Babylon to place the golden crown upon his own head as Nebuchadnezzar I.

19
00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:12.360
[George] Nebuchadnezzar was not merely a brilliant general, he was the greatest urban builder of antiquity.

20
00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:19.800
[George] Over his forty-three year reign, he poured the spoils of empire into transforming Babylon into a shimmering wonder of the world.

21
00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:33.480
[George] He enclosed the city within a double ring of colossal mud brick and kiln fired walls 50 feet high and so wide that Greek historian Herodotus noted two four horse chariots could pass each other atop the ramparts without scraping.

22
00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:40.920
[George] The ceremonial jewel of the city was the Ishtar Gate, the north entrance dedicated to the goddess of love and war.

23
00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:53.680
[George] German archaeologist Robert Coldwy excavated the gate in 1899, uncovering thousands of vibrant, glazed ceramic bricks fired in deep cobalt blue resembling precious lapis lazuli.

24
00:03:53.680 --> 00:04:11.200
[George] The gate was adorned with magnificent, raised yellow and white reliefs of 575 sacred animals, fierce golden lions representing Ishtar, serpentine dragons named Mushhushu representing Marduk, and muscular young bulls representing the weather god Adad.

25
00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:30.960
[George] Through this gate ran the majestic processional way, paved with red breccia and white limestone slabs, leading to the colossal ziggurat etamenanki, the temple of the foundation of heaven and earth, revered in world folklore as the Tower of Babel, soaring three hundred feet into the turquoise Mesopotamian sky.

26
00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:39.520
[George] Among the legendary architectural achievements of Nebuchadnezzar was one of the seven wonders of the ancient world, the hanging gardens of Babylon.

27
00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:47.440
[George] To seal his crucial military alliance with the Medes, Nebuchadnezzar had married Princess Amates, the daughter of Syaksares.

28
00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:56.960
[George] Amates had grown up in the rugged, forested mountains of Media, surrounded by misty green peaks, roaring waterfalls, and fragrant mountain meadows.

29
00:04:56.960 --> 00:05:04.560
[George] In contrast, the sun baked, flat alluvial plain of Mesopotamia was a monotonous landscape of yellow mud and dust.

30
00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:09.040
[George] Homesick and depressed, the young queen wept for her green homeland.

31
00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:15.680
[George] To comfort his beloved bride, Nebuchadnezzar performed an astonishing act of royal romantic devotion.

32
00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:19.200
[George] He built an artificial mountain inside the palace walls.

33
00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:30.000
[George] Soaring seventy-five feet into the sky on vaulted brick arches, the gardens consisted of tiered stone terraces waterproofed with thick layers of reed, bitumen, and lead sheets.

34
00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:38.920
[George] Hundreds of exotic flowering trees, climbing vines, and fragrant shrubs collected from across the empire were planted in deep soil.

35
00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:54.520
[George] Babylonian hydraulic engineers devised an ingenious system of Archimedes screws and continuous bucket chain pumps to hoist tons of freshwater from the Euphrates River to the highest terrace, creating cascading waterfalls and fountains that cooled the desert air.

36
00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:59.160
[George] Relying an enchanted mountain paradise floating above the shimmering city.

37
00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:06.760
[George] In the second year of his reign, Nebuchadnezzar was tormented by a terrifying, recurring nightmare that robbed him of sleep.

38
00:06:06.760 --> 00:06:15.640
[George] Summoning his court magicians, astrologers, sorcerers, and Chaldean wise men, the king made an impossible, authoritarian demand.

39
00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:23.480
[George] Tell me the dream that I have forgotten, and the interpretation thereof, or ye shall be cut in pieces and your houses made a dunghill.

40
00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:29.960
[George] The magicians gasped in despair, answering, There is not a man upon the earth that can show the king's matter.

41
00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:34.040
[George] Therefore, there is no king that asked such things of any magician.

42
00:06:34.040 --> 00:06:39.720
[George] Furious, Nebuchadnezzar decreed the immediate execution of all wise men in Babylon.

43
00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:50.920
[George] Enter twenty-year-old Daniel, a noble Jewish youth taken captive from Jerusalem who had refused to defile himself with the king's rich meat and wine, flourishing on pulse and water.

44
00:06:50.920 --> 00:06:56.200
[George] Daniel prayed to the God of heaven, and the secret was revealed to him in a night vision.

45
00:06:56.200 --> 00:07:01.480
[George] Standing boldly before the golden throne, Daniel revealed the forgotten nightmare.

46
00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:07.800
[George] Nebuchadnezzar had seen a colossal, terrifying statue standing before him in dazzling brilliance.

47
00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:18.720
[George] Its head was of fine gold, its chest and arms of silver, its belly and thighs of bronze, its legs of iron, and its feet partly of iron and partly of baked clay.

48
00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:33.840
[George] Then, a supernatural stone cut out without hands struck the statue on its clay feet, smashing the entire image to fine dust that was blown away like summer chaff on the thrashing floor, while the stone became a great mountain that filled the whole earth.

49
00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:43.120
[George] Daniel looked straight into the eyes of the master of the ancient world and delivered an interpretation that laid out the entire future history of Western civilization.

50
00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:50.080
[George] Thou, O king, art a king of kings, for the God of heaven hath given thee a kingdom, power and strength.

51
00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:53.360
[George] And glory, thou art this head of gold.

52
00:07:53.360 --> 00:08:02.400
[George] But Daniel warned that human glory was fleeting, after Babylon would arise an inferior second kingdom of silver, the Medo-Persian Empire.

53
00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:15.680
[George] Followed by a third kingdom of bronze that would rule the whole earth, the Greek Empire of Alexander the Great, followed by a fourth kingdom strong as iron that breaks and bruises all things, the brutal Roman Empire.

54
00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:26.000
[George] Finally, the divided feet of iron and clay represented the fractured nations of modern Europe, partly strong and partly brittle, never truly cleaving to one another.

55
00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:31.600
[George] And in the days of these kings shall the God of heaven set up a kingdom, which shall never be destroyed.

56
00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:39.760
[George] And the kingdom shall not be left to other people, but it shall break in pieces and consume all these kingdoms, and it shall stand for ever.

57
00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:55.200
[George] Overcome with awe and spiritual terror, the mighty emperor fell on his face before the young Jewish captive, worshipping Daniel and declaring, Of a truth it is, that your God is a god of gods, and a lord of kings, and a revealer of secrets.

58
00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:59.920
[George] Nebuchadnezzar promoted Daniel to chief governor over the whole province of Babylon.

59
00:09:00.200 --> 00:09:06.920
[George] Yet Nebuchadnezzar's imperial ego could not long accept the prophecy that his golden kingdom would pass away.

60
00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:25.080
[George] Defying the vision of the multi-metal statue, Nebuchadnezzar erected an immense statue entirely of pure gold, sixty cubits high and six cubits wide, on the flat plain of Dura outside Babylon, summoning thousands of princes, governors, captains, and judges from across the empire.

61
00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:38.920
[George] The royal herald proclaimed that at the sound of the cornet, flute, harp, sackbutt, and psaltery, every citizen must fall flat and worship the golden image, or be cast immediately into the midst of a burning fiery furnace.

62
00:09:38.920 --> 00:09:47.000
[George] Three young Jewish officials appointed by Daniel, Shadrach, Meshach, and Abidnego, stood tall and refused to bow.

63
00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:55.640
[George] Hauled before the enraged monarch, they delivered words of unyielding faith, our God whom we serve is able to deliver us from the burning fiery furnace.

64
00:09:55.640 --> 00:10:04.360
[George] But if not, be it known unto thee, O king, that we will not serve thy gods, nor worship the golden image which thou hast set up.

65
00:10:04.360 --> 00:10:13.880
[George] Nebuchadnezzar was full of fury, ordering the furnace heated seven times hotter than normal, so intense that the flames killed the soldiers who threw them in.

66
00:10:13.880 --> 00:10:18.200
[George] But as the king watched from his throne, he leaped up in astonishment.

67
00:10:18.200 --> 00:10:21.880
[George] Did not we cast three men bound into the midst of the fire?

68
00:10:21.880 --> 00:10:30.200
[George] Lo, I see four men loose, walking in the midst of the fire, and they have no hurt, and the form of the fourth is like the Son of God.

69
00:10:30.200 --> 00:10:38.120
[George] Faith had triumphed over royal tyranny, demonstrating that the God of heaven ruled supreme over the greatest monarchs of antiquity.

70
00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:47.200
[Rebecca] Nebuchadnezzar built the golden head of ancient empires, but his imperial pride was destined for a profound, humbling reckoning.

71
00:10:47.200 --> 00:10:51.600
[Rebecca] Next week we conclude our Babylonian saga with episode 205.

72
00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:54.080
[Rebecca] The fall of Babylon and the Writing on the Wall.

73
00:10:54.080 --> 00:11:10.880
[Rebecca] Nebuchadnezzar's descent into madness eating grass like an ox, the destruction of Solomon's temple, Belshazzar's sacrilegious banquet drinking from the holy vessels, and the mysterious hand that wrote mean, mean, tekal, a parson in fire as Cyrus the Great diverted the Euphrates River.

74
00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:15.440
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

